{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788491849-eDapygcuCJMfk8xJnU9H","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting — still failing. Why? Because `url_to_fs` passes the urlpath positionally AND the kwargs contain `url`. Wait — the error is `url_to_fs() got multiple values for argument 'url'`, which happens inside `url_to_fs`... no wait, the traceback says line 5 which is the `url_to_fs(...)` call itself. Hmm, `url_to_fs(url, **kwargs)` — first param is named `url`! So passing `url=` as a kwarg to `url_to_fs` conflicts with `url_to_fs`'s own `url` parameter!\n\nWait, but in the HF datasets case: `url_to_fs(output_dir, **(storage_options or {}))` where output_dir = \"dvc://workshop/satellite-data/jan_train.csv\" (positional) and storage_options={\"url\": ...}. So `url` in storage_options directly conflicts with `url_to_fs`'s signature. Hmm, but the fix upstream was to rename the DVCFileSystem param... so how does that fix the HF case?\n\nLet me look at how fsspec's `url_to_fs` handles kwargs... In `url_to_fs`:\n\n```python\ndef url_to_fs(url, **kwargs):\n    url = stringify_path(url)\n    known_kwargs = {...}\n    kwargs = {k: v for k, v in kwargs.items() if k not in known_kwargs}\n    chain = _un_chain(url, kwargs)\n    inkwargs = {}\n    for i, ch in enumerate(reversed(chain)):\n        urls, protocol, kw = ch\n        if i == len(chain) - 1:\n            inkwargs = dict(**kw, **inkwargs)\n            continue\n        ...\n    urlpath, protocol, _ = chain[0]\n    fs = filesystem(protocol, **inkwargs)\n    return fs, urlpath\n```\n\nHmm — `_un_chain(url, kwargs)` — let me look at `_un_chain`. It splits chained URLs like `simplecache::zip://file.zip::https://...`. For a plain `dvc://...` URL, the chain is one element and kwargs are... let me check `_un_chain`: it takes kwargs and pops \"storage_options\"? Let me look at the fsspec source.\n\nActually wait — in my test, the error occurred at the `url_to_fs(...)` call site itself: \"url_to_fs() got multiple values for argument 'url'\". That's because `url_to_fs(url, url=...)` — Python binds `url=` to the first parameter `url`, and I also passed positionally. Right — so `storage_options={\"url\": ...}` passed by datasets → `url_to_fs(output_dir, **storage_options)` → `url_to_fs(output_dir, url=...)` → TypeError, since `output_dir` is positional-first bound to `url` param.\n\nHmm, so renaming DVCFileSystem's param doesn't fix that at the `url_to_fs` level?! Unless... fsspec's `url_to_fs` filters kwargs before... no, the TypeError occurs at call time, before any filtering.\n\nWait, let me re-read the traceback from the issue:\n\n```\n  File \"...\\datasets\\load.py\", line 2151, in load_dataset\n    builder_instance.download_and_prepare(\n  File \"...\\datasets\\builder.py\", line 80...\n    fs, output_dir = url_to_fs(output_dir, **(storage_options or {}))\nTypeError: url_to_fs() got multiple values for argument 'url'\n```\n\nSo indeed the error is in `url_to_fs(output_dir, **storage_options)`. Hmm. So how would renaming DVCFileSystem's `url` param to `repo` fix this? It wouldn't at the call level...\n\nHmm wait — maybe newer fsspec versions changed `url_to_fs` to accept `path` or handle this. Let me check the installed fsspec 2025.7.0's url_to_fs signature: `def url_to_fs(url, **kwargs)`. Still `url` first param.\n\nHmm, so with fsspec 2025.7.0 and dvc 3.59.1+... the user passes `storage_options={\"repo\": \"https://github.com/...\"}` instead of `{\"url\": ...}`. So the fix requires the USER to change their storage_options key from \"url\" to \"repo\". The DVC change makes `repo=` the documented, canonical way (matching dvc.api.open/read), while `url=` remains supported for backward compat (for cases where `url` isn't swallowed by url_to_fs's own signature, e.g. `fsspec.filesystem(\"dvc\", url=...)` or DVCFileSystem(url=...) direct calls).\n\nSo the correct new usage is:\n\n```python\ndataset = load_dataset(\n    \"cs