y: `filesystem(protocol, **inkwargs)`... hmm, `filesystem(protocol, **inkwargs)` — is urlpath passed positionally? Looking at url_to_fs source:\n\n```python\n    urlpath, protocol, _ = chain[0]\n    fs = filesystem(protocol, **inkwargs)\n    return fs, urlpath\n```\n\nNo! The urlpath is NOT passed to the filesystem — it's returned separately. So `fs = filesystem('dvc', repo='https://github.com/...')` → `_DVCFileSystem(repo='https://github.com/iterative/dataset-registry.git')`. The path comes back separately. That's how fsspec works — the path is separate.\n\nSo before the change: `url_to_fs('dvc://...', repo='https://...')` → `_DVCFileSystem(repo='https://...')` — repo param expected a Repo instance but got a string → breaks (or with `url=`: TypeError multiple values). After the change: `repo='https://...'` is a string → `os.fspath` → `_repo_kwargs['url'] = 'https://...'`. \n\nAnd in test 1, the printed path was `workshop/satellite-data/jan_train.csv` — hmm, note the leading slash got stripped: `dvc://workshop/...` → path `workshop/...`. Hmm, `DVCFileSystem._strip_protocol`... whatever, that's pre-existing behavior.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety — in test 1, `_repo_kwargs` printed? No, it failed before printing because `fs.fs` doesn't exist (fs IS `_DVCFileSystem`). Let me redo the verification with correct attribute access.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"SiliconFlow","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ced045f-2deb-4a03-96b6-51f3c49ecc7b","uuid":"2e915ba2-e6bf-42f8-913b-5366ae4d6ef4","timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:19:25.159Z","request_id":"gen-1788491870-fWed8BX7p9YvXpVkaqUG"}
